 ✅  What you really need to do
Enable the Ubuntu “universe” repository (many missing packages belong there).
Install the language runtimes you’ll use – Rust, Go, Node / npm, Python + pipx.
Install the packages that are in the Ubuntu repos (single apt command).
Install the Snap‑only tools (if you want them).
Install the rest with the appropriate language‑specific installer (cargo, go, npm, pipx).
Add the binary directories to $PATH once, then you’re done.
Below is a complete, copy‑paste‑ready script that does exactly that on an Ubuntu 22.04 (or newer) WSL distribution.
If you prefer doing each step manually, the script is fully commented so you can copy only the sections you need.

📦 Full installation script for Ubuntu WSL
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# =============================================================================
#  install_all.sh – One‑shot installer for the gigantic toolbox you posted.
#  Run on a **fresh** Ubuntu (or Ubuntu‑based) WSL distribution.
# =============================================================================
set -euo pipefail
IFS=$'\n\t'

# ------------------------------- 0️⃣ PRE‑CHECKS -------------------------------
# Make sure we have sudo rights
if ! sudo -v; then
  echo "❌  sudo is required – aborting."
  exit 1
fi

# ------------------------------- 1️⃣ ENABLE REPOS -------------------------------
log() { echo -e "\e[32m[+] $*\e[0m"; }
warn(){ echo -e "\e[33m[!] $*\e[0m"; }

log "Enabling the Ubuntu 'universe' repository (many packages live there)…"
sudo add-apt-repository -y universe
# Azure CLI, Helm, etc. need extra PPAs – we’ll add them later
sudo apt-get update -y

# ------------------------------- 2️⃣ CORE DEV TOOLS -------------------------------
log "Installing generic build tools, curl, git, locales…"
sudo apt-get install -y \
  build-essential curl wget git ca-certificates gnupg2 lsb-release \
  software-properties-common \
  locales

# Make sure your locale is set (WSL sometimes defaults to C.UTF-8)
sudo locale-gen en_US.UTF-8
export LANG=en_US.UTF-8

# ------------------------------- 3️⃣ LANGUAGE RUNTIMES -------------------------------
# ------- Rust (cargo) -------------------------------------------------
if ! command -v cargo >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing Rust via rustup…"
  curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh -s -- -y
  source "$HOME/.cargo/env"
else
  log "Rust already installed (cargo version: $(cargo --version))"
fi

# ------- Go -------------------------------------------------------------
if ! command -v go >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing Go (apt provides a recent enough version)…"
  sudo apt-get install -y golang
else
  log "Go already present (go version: $(go version))"
fi

# ------- Node.js + npm (use Node 20 LTS) ---------------------------------
if ! command -v node >/dev/null; then
  log "Adding NodeSource repo for Node 20 LTS…"
  curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash -
  sudo apt-get install -y nodejs
else
  log "Node already installed (node –v: $(node -v))"
fi

# ------- Python 3 + pipx -----------------------------------------------
if ! command -v pip3 >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing Python3 + pip…"
  sudo apt-get install -y python3 python3-pip python3-venv
fi
if ! command -v pipx >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing pipx (isolated Python‑CLI installer)…"
  python3 -m pip install --user pipx
  python3 -m pipx ensurepath
else
  log "pipx already present (pipx version: $(pipx --version))"
fi

# Ensure the newly‑created binary dirs are in PATH for the remainder of this script
export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/go/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

# ------------------------------- 4️⃣ APT PACKAGES (available in Ubuntu) -------------------------------
log "Installing the subset of tools that exist in the official Ubuntu repos…"

APT_PKGS=(
  # System / monitoring
  fd-find  # installed as `fd`
  ripgrep
  fzf
  bat
  exa          # Ubuntu calls it `exa`; we’ll alias later
  eza          # newer replacement (already installed as `eza`)
  ncdu
  htop
  iotop
  sysstat
  duf
  dust
  duf
  lsof
  smartmontools    # provides `smartctl`
  # Filesystem / search
  fd-find          # already listed, keeps it safe
  locate           # `mlocate`
  gnupg2
  ssh
  rsync
  # Compression / archiving
  p7zip-full
  unzip
  zip
  zstd
  # Media tools
  ffmpeg
  imagemagick      # `magick` command
  # Development / git helpers
  git
  tig
  jq
  yq
  # Container / k8s helper binaries that are *in* Ubuntu
  kubectl
  # Misc
  curl
  wget
  tree
  zip
  unzip
  # Snap (see next section) – we just install the snap package manager itself
  snapd
)

sudo apt-get install -y "${APT_PKGS[@]}"

# Cleanup duplicate names (`fd-find` provides `fd`) – add an alias:
if ! command -v fd >/dev/null; then
  sudo ln -s "$(command -v fdfind)" /usr/local/bin/fd
fi

# ------------------------------- 5️⃣ SNAP PACKAGES (newer binaries) -------------------------------
log "Installing a handful of tools that are only shipped as Snap packages…"

# Make sure snapd is running (WSL can use snap without GUI)
sudo systemctl enable --now snapd.socket || true   # ignore error on WSL (systemd may not be active)

SNAP_PKGS=(
  starship   # modern prompt, also available via apt but snap gives newest
  eza        # fast ls replacement (if you want the real `eza` binary)
  k9s        # Kubernetes UI
  bat        # (the snap version is usually newer than apt)
  bottom     # (if you prefer a Snap of bottom; otherwise we’ll use cargo)
)

for pkg in "${SNAP_PKGS[@]}"; do
  if ! snap list "$pkg" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log "Snap‑installing $pkg…"
    sudo snap install "$pkg" --classic || warn "snap install $pkg failed"
  else
    log "Snap $pkg already installed"
  fi
done

# ------------------------------- 6️⃣ RUST CRATES (cargo install) -------------------------------
log "Installing the Rust‑based tools that are NOT in apt…"

CARGO_PKGS=(
  procs
  gotop
  bottom          # (we also installed via snap – cargo will overwrite with latest)
  zenith
  fkill-cli
  joker
  starship        # already via snap (skip if you prefer snap)
  ripgrep         # already via apt (skip if you want newest)
  fd-find
  duf
  dust
  bat             # we already have snap/bat; install latest via cargo
  hyperfine
  aichat
  catai
  chatblade
  gpt-engineer
  gptscript
  gptui
  how2
  # AI/LLM helpers that ship as Rust crates
  # (if any of them are missing from crates.io, we’ll fall back later)
)

for crate in "${CARGO_PKGS[@]}"; do
  bin_name="$crate"
  # Some crates have a different binary name
  case "$crate" in
    fd-find)   bin_name="fd" ;;
    bat)       bin_name="batcat" ;;   # Ubuntu's `bat` binary is `batcat`
  esac

  if command -v "$bin_name" >/dev/null; then
    log "✔ $bin_name already in PATH – skipping cargo install."
    continue
  fi

  log "cargo installing $crate…"
  cargo install "$crate" || warn "cargo install $crate failed"
done

# ------------------------------- 7️⃣ GO TOOLS (go install) -------------------------------
log "Installing Go‑based utilities (via go install) …"

GO_PKGS=(
  # format: binary=module@version
  "croc=github.com/schollz/croc/v9@latest"
  "gdu=github.com/dundee/gdu@latest"
  "lazydocker=github.com/jesseduffield/lazydocker@latest"
  "dive=github.com/wagoodman/dive@latest"
  "ghz=github.com/bojand/ghz@latest"
)

for entry in "${GO_PKGS[@]}"; do
  IFS="=" read -r bin mod <<<"$entry"
  if command -v "$bin" >/dev/null; then
    log "✔ $bin already installed – skipping."
    continue
  fi
  log "go install $mod → $bin"
  GO111MODULE=on go install "$mod"
done

# ------------------------------- 8️⃣ NPM GLOBAL PACKAGES -------------------------------
log "Installing Node‑based CLIs globally (npm) …"

NPM_PKGS=(
  # The list you gave contained only `fkill-cli` (already a Rust crate)
  # Add any other npm‑only tools here, e.g.
  # "npkill" "http-server"
)

for pkg in "${NPM_PKGS[@]}"; do
  if npm list -g --depth=0 "$pkg" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    log "✔ npm package $pkg already installed"
    continue
  fi
  log "npm installing $pkg globally…"
  sudo npm install -g "$pkg" || warn "npm install $pkg failed"
done

# ------------------------------- 9️⃣ PIPX PYTHON TOOLS -------------------------------
log "Installing Python‑only CLI tools via pipx …"

PIPX_PKGS=(
  aichat
  catai
  chatblade
  gpt-engineer
  gptscript
  gptui
  how2
  huggingface-cli
  llm
  open-interpreter
  whispercpp
  chatgpt
  xlsxsql
  sd
  # Misc python tools that are not in apt
  # (coala, etc. can be installed here if you prefer pipx to apt)
)

for pkg in "${PIPX_PKGS[@]}"; do
  if pipx list | grep -q "^$pkg "; then
    log "✔ $pkg already installed via pipx"
    continue
  fi
  log "pipx installing $pkg…"
  pipx install "$pkg" || warn "pipx install $pkg failed"
done

# ------------------------------- 10️⃣ CLOUD PROVIDER CLIs (official upstream installers) -------------------------------
log "Installing cloud‑provider CLIs that are not in Ubuntu repos…"

# ── AWS CLI (v2) ──────────────────────
if ! command -v aws >/dev/null; then
  log "Downloading & installing AWS CLI v2…"
  curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "/tmp/awscliv2.zip"
  unzip -q /tmp/awscliv2.zip -d /tmp
  sudo /tmp/aws/install
else
  log "AWS CLI already present (aws – version: $(aws --version))"
fi

# ── Azure CLI (Microsoft repo) ────────
if ! command -v az >/dev/null; then
  log "Adding Microsoft repo and installing azure‑cli…"
  curl -sL https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc | sudo apt-key add -
  sudo add-apt-repository "deb [arch=amd64] https://packages.microsoft.com/repos/azure-cli/ $(lsb_release -cs) main"
  sudo apt-get update -y
  sudo apt-get install -y azure-cli
else
  log "Azure CLI already present (az version: $(az version | head -n1))"
fi

# ── IBM Cloud CLI ─────────────────────
if ! command -v ibmcloud >/dev/null; then
  log "Downloading IBM Cloud CLI…"
  curl -fsSL https://clis.cloud.ibm.com/install/linux | sh
fi

# ── DigitalOcean (`doctl`) ─────────────
if ! command -v doctl >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing doctl (DigitalOcean CLI)…"
  curl -sL https://github.com/digitalocean/doctl/releases/latest/download/doctl-$(uname -s)-$(uname -m).tar.gz | \
    tar -xzv -C /tmp
  sudo mv /tmp/doctl /usr/local/bin/
fi

# ── Hetzner Cloud (`hcloud`) ───────────
if ! command -v hcloud >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing hcloud CLI…"
  curl -L https://github.com/hetznercloud/cli/releases/latest/download/hcloud-linux-amd64.tar.gz | \
    tar -xz -C /tmp
  sudo mv /tmp/hcloud /usr/local/bin/
fi

# ── Other provider CLIs (fastly, scaleway, vultr, …)
# Each of them typically provides a single binary release on GitHub.
# Below is an example helper – you can copy‑paste it for any missing tool:
#
# install_from_github() {
#   local name=$1 url=$2
#   if ! command -v "$name" >/dev/null; then
#     log "Installing $name from $url …"
#     curl -L "$url" -o "/tmp/${name}.tar.gz"
#     tar -xzf "/tmp/${name}.tar.gz" -C "/tmp"
#     sudo mv "/tmp/${name}" "/usr/local/bin/"
#   else
#     log "$name already present"
#   fi
# }
# install_from_github fastly "https://github.com/fastly/cli/releases/latest/download/fastly_$(uname -s)_$(uname -m).tar.gz"
# … (repeat for every provider you need)

# ------------------------------- 11️⃣ HELM (official script) -------------------------------
if ! command -v helm >/dev/null; then
  log "Installing Helm via the official script…"
  curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash
else
  log "Helm already installed (helm version: $(helm version --short))"
fi

# ------------------------------- 12️⃣ KUBECTL, KIND, K3D, MINIKUBE -------------------------------
# kubectl is already installed via apt above; for the rest use their official binaries:

install_binary() {
  local name=$1 url=$2
  if ! command -v "$name" >/dev/null; then
    log "Downloading $name…"
    curl -L "$url" -o "/tmp/$name"
    chmod +x "/tmp/$name"
    sudo mv "/tmp/$name" "/usr/local/bin/$name"
  else
    log "$name already present"
  fi
}

# kind (Kubernetes in Docker)
install_binary kind "https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/dl/v0.23.0/kind-$(uname -s)-$(uname -m)"

# k3d (K3s in Docker)
install_binary k3d "https://github.com/k3d-io/k3d/releases/latest/download/k3d-$(uname -s)-$(uname -m)"

# minikube
install_binary minikube "https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64"

# ------------------------------- 13️⃣ FINAL PATH TWEAKS -------------------------------
# If you sourced the script from a login shell, your PATH is already updated.
# To make the changes permanent, append the following line to ~/.bashrc (or ~/.zshrc):
echo 'export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/go/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.bashrc
log "PATH export added to ~/.bashrc – run 'source ~/.bashrc' or start a new terminal."

# ------------------------------- 14️⃣ DONE! -------------------------------
log "✅  All requested tools have been processed."
log "Run 'which <tool>' or '<tool> --version' to verify any individual binary."
log "If you still see “command not found” for a particular tool, it probably"
log "needs a manual download (e.g., a GUI‑only binary) – let me know and I’ll help."

exit 0
How to run the script
# 1️⃣ Save it:
cat > ~/install_all.sh <<'EOS'
# (paste the whole script above)
EOS

# 2️⃣ Make it executable
chmod +x ~/install_all.sh

# 3️⃣ Run it (it will ask for your sudo password a few times)
./install_all.sh
The script:

Stops on the first error (set -e). If you want it to keep going, replace set -e with set +e (but you’ll have to watch the warnings).
Logs every major step in green, warnings in yellow, so you can see what succeeded and what didn’t.
Leaves the system in a clean state – all binaries are in $HOME/.cargo/bin, $HOME/go/bin or $HOME/.local/bin, which are now permanently added to your $PATH.
📚 What to do if you still get “package not found”
Missing package	Where to look / how to install
procs, gotop, bottom, zenith, fkill-cli, joker, osquery	cargo (cargo install <crate>) – already handled in the script.
fastfetch, macchina, ismc, viddy, sampler	Most are Rust crates – cargo install fastfetch, etc.
aichat, catai, chatblade, gpt‑engineer, gptscript, gptui, how2, huggingface-cli, llm, open‑interpreter, whispercpp, chatgpt, xlsxsql, sd	Python – pipx install <name> (script does this).
awscli	sudo apt-get install awscli works on Ubuntu 22.04 LTS if the “universe” repo is enabled. If you prefer the newest v2, use the official bundled installer (script does this).
azure-cli	Needs the Microsoft APT repo (added by the script).
exif, exiftool, pdfcpu, pdfminer	Usually Python: pipx install pdfcpu (if a binary exists) or pip install pdfminer.six.
starship, eza, k9s	Snap – sudo snap install starship, etc. (script does it).
helm	Official install script (`curl …
kubectx/kubens	Not in Ubuntu’s base repo – install via snap install kubectx or clone the repo (git clone https://github.com/ahmetb/kubectx.git $HOME/.kubectx && sudo ln -s $HOME/.kubectx/kubectx /usr/local/bin/kubectx).
kind, k3d, minikube	Binary releases – the script downloads the latest stable version.
coala	Not packaged – pipx install coala (or pip install coala).
gifski	Not in Ubuntu 22.04 – cargo install gifski (or use the pre‑built binary from GitHub).
cloud provider CLIs (Fastly, Scaleway, Vultr, JDCloud, etc.)	Usually a single binary release on GitHub – use the install_from_github helper in the script or add the same pattern yourself.
Any other tool	Search “ Ubuntu install” or “ GitHub releases”. Most open‑source CLIs provide a pre‑built tarball or a cargo/go/npm/pipx install method.
