🎯 What to remember
apt can only give you what Ubuntu ships – everything else needs a language‑specific installer or a direct binary download.
Enable the universe repo (and add third‑party repos for Azure CLI, Helm, etc.).
Install the language runtimes first (rustup, go, node, pipx) – they are the “package managers for the rest”.
Prefer cargo install for Rust tools (procs, gotop, bottom, zenith, fkill-cli, …).
Prefer go install for Go tools (croc, gdu, lazydocker, …).
Prefer npm -g for JavaScript/Node tools (if any).
Prefer pipx install for Python‑only CLIs (AI/LLM helpers, coala, etc.).
Use snap for things that only exist as snap packages (starship, eza, k9s).
Add the respective binary directories to $PATH (the script does it for you).
Once you have those runtimes in place, adding any new CLI from this list in the future is as easy as:

cargo install <crate>          # Rust
go install <module>@latest    # Go
npm install -g <package>      # Node
pipx install <package>        # Python
snap install <snap-name>      # Snap
That’s the canonical, maintainable workflow for a massive, cross‑language toolbox on Ubuntu WSL.

